a isibhengezo esicenga ngayo", "subject": "isiXhosa HL", "grade": "Grade 11", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-xhhl-11.pdf", "bytes": 69058, "sha256": "ec14a6bed037e0d1f56baa289e7c2c9267f9fdbc92379a8bbf81eaca72511223", "topic": "Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetyenziswa kolwimi", "language": "isiXhosa", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=QsGQeCZMUxo%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1555", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "xhhl-12", "title": "Yakha umhlathi wohlalutyo loncwadi", "subject": "isiXhosa HL", "grade": "Grade 12", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-xhhl-12.pdf", "bytes": 69063, "sha256": "e0350af93f15159b46d18f512d51056675209e088b987ebebc46b702dd9e3d35", "topic": "Ukufunda nokubukela: uncwadi; ukubhala nokunikezela", "language": "isiXhosa", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=QsGQeCZMUxo%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1555", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "zuhl-10", "title": "Ukufunda ngokuqondisisa", "subject": "isiZulu HL", "grade": "Grade 10", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-zuhl-10.pdf", "bytes": 68771, "sha256": "ba1d4fecc827b7b85805951aa50d5f73d8b032a01b2a19815cf509e0e42cdde8", "topic": "Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqondisisa; ulimi engqikithini", "language": "isiZulu", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=nZlas4HGdYI%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1555", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "zuhl-11", "title": "Hlaziya ulimi lokunxenxa", "subject": "isiZulu HL", "grade": "Grade 11", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-zuhl-11.pdf", "bytes": 69042, "sha256": "48723debf4c77cb2f0da9b19c56febd3d06b5b2cc61056b159fa1729db1631fa", "topic": "Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetshenziswa kolimi", "language": "isiZulu", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=nZlas4HGdYI%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1555", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "zuhl-12", "title": "Bhala isigaba sokuhlaziya izinkondlo", "subject": "isiZulu HL", "grade": "Grade 12", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-zuhl-12.pdf", "bytes": 68997, "sha256": "5b2c1c3a02e62a280963bf7c55ab02290c6d963730fffe12fac3069db128d185", "topic": "Ukufunda nokubukela: imibhalo yobuciko; ukubhala nokwethula", "language": "isiZulu", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=nZlas4HGdYI%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1555", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "lo-10", "title": "Set a goal you can act on", "subject": "Life Orientation", "grade": "Grade 10", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-lo-10.pdf", "bytes": 69180, "sha256": "3b59ec921ccc9b35830decb1a8b8dffa936c66a3e47c322ac13ad8e6dc0b5ef5", "topic": "Development of the self in society: self-awareness and goal setting", "language": "English", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=aovsPAsVZao%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1558", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "lo-11", "title": "Make an informed study-path decision", "subject": "Life Orientation", "grade": "Grade 11", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-lo-11.pdf", "bytes": 68993, "sha256": "e6871c1bf667fa3b52c096e2dd44aa3a722296f4f2760c6b7d518b68d964d493", "topic": "Careers and career choices: requirements and opportunities", "language": "English", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=aovsPAsVZao%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1558", "edition": "2026.1"}, {"id": "lo-12", "title": "Prepare for assessments sustainably", "subject": "Life Orientation", "grade": "Grade 12", "url": "/study-packs/smartbridge-lo-12.pdf", "bytes": 69067, "sha256": "32fce820fd90f2f3a3ae858fcd442cdbfe26bf70bb873445885d1d380032bcdc", "topic": "Study skills: examination preparation and stress management", "language": "English", "source": "https://www.education.gov.za/LinkClick.aspx?fileticket=aovsPAsVZao%3d&tabid=570&portalid=0&mid=1558", "edition": "2026.1"}]$catalogue$::jsonb) as x(id text,title text,subject text,grade text,url text,bytes integer,sha256 text,topic text,language text,source text,edition text)
where not exists(select 1 from public.resources r where r.metadata->>'smartbridge_pack_id'=x.id);

-- Index the original guide text; preserve all pre-existing publisher passages.
insert into public.learning_knowledge_chunks(source_type,source_id,title,subject,grade,page_number,chunk_number,content)
select 'resources',r.id,r.title,r.subject,r.grade,c.page,c.number,c.content
from jsonb_to_recordset($chunks$[{"pack_id": "math-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Algebraic expressions; equations and inequalities. Factorising reverses multiplication. Always check for a common factor before trying brackets. For x² + bx + c, find two numbers with sum b and product c. This shortcut assumes the coefficient of x² is 1. The zero-product rule applies to a product equal to zero: AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0."}, {"pack_id": "math-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Algebraic expressions; equations and inequalities. Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0. The numbers −2 and −3 have sum −5 and product 6. Write (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0. Set each bracket equal to zero. x = 2 or x = 3. Check: 4 − 10 + 6 = 0 and 9 − 15 + 6 = 0."}, {"pack_id": "math-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Algebraic expressions; equations and inequalities. 1. Factorise 6x² + 9x. Answer: 3x(2x + 3). The common factor is 3x. 2. Solve x² + 7x + 12 = 0. Answer: (x + 3)(x + 4) = 0, so x = −3 or −4. 3. A learner says x² = 9 gives only x = 3. Correct the answer and explain. Answer: x = 3 or −3, because squaring either number gives 9."}, {"pack_id": "math-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Functions: quadratic functions. A quadratic graph has equation y = ax² + bx + c, with a ≠ 0. Positive a opens upwards; negative a opens downwards. The axis of symmetry is x = −b/(2a). Substitute this x-value to find the turning point. The y-intercept occurs at x = 0. The x-intercepts occur where y = 0; there may be two, one or no real roots."}, {"pack_id": "math-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Functions: quadratic functions. Sketch y = x² − 4x + 3 and state its range. Factorise: y = (x − 1)(x − 3), giving roots 1 and 3. The axis is x = 2; y(2) = −1. Mark (2; −1) and the y-intercept (0; 3). Draw a smooth upward parabola through these points. The range is y ≥ −1."}, {"pack_id": "math-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Functions: quadratic functions. 1. Find the roots of y = x² − 6x + 8. Answer: (x − 2)(x − 4) gives x = 2 and x = 4. 2. Find its turning point. Answer: x = 3; y = 9 − 18 + 8 = −1. Turning point (3; −1). 3. State its axis of symmetry and range. Answer: Axis x = 3; range y ≥ −1."}, {"pack_id": "math-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Differential calculus: derivatives; cubic functions. The derivative f′(x) describes the gradient of the tangent. For a power axⁿ, the derivative is anxⁿ⁻¹. At a stationary point f′(x) = 0. Use f(x), not f′(x), to find the point’s y-coordinate. A change in f′ from positive to negative indicates a local maximum; negative to positive indicates a local minimum."}, {"pack_id": "math-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Differential calculus: derivatives; cubic functions. Find and classify the stationary points of f(x) = x³ − 3x. f′(x) = 3x² − 3. Set 3(x² − 1) = 0, so x = −1 or 1. f(−1) = 2 and f(1) = −2. The points are (−1; 2) and (1; −2). The derivative changes + to − at −1: local maximum. It changes − to + at 1: local minimum."}, {"pack_id": "math-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Differential calculus: derivatives; cubic functions. 1. Differentiate 2x³ − 5x² + 4. Answer: 6x² − 10x. The derivative of the constant is zero. 2. Find the gradient of its tangent at x = 2. Answer: 6(2)² − 10(2) = 4. 3. Find the tangent equation there. Answer: f(2) = 0, so y − 0 = 4(x − 2), giving y = 4x − 8."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: income, expenditure and budgets. A budget estimates money received and spent over the same period. Actual spending may differ from planned spending. Fixed expenses are usually agreed amounts; variable expenses depend on use. A surplus is income minus expenses when the result is positive. Compare like periods and units. Include transport, food and data before treating an apparent surplus as available savings."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: income, expenditure and budgets. Use the plan to calculate the balance and test whether saving R250 is feasible. Expenses = 400 + 350 + 150 + 100 = R1 000. Balance = R1 200 − R1 000 = R200. Saving R250 needs another R50. Reduce an affordable expense or increase income; do not assume essential transport can disappear."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: income, expenditure and budgets. 1. Income is R2 000 and expenses R1 750. Calculate the surplus. Answer: R250. 2. Data spending rises from R150 to R180. Find the increase as a percentage. Answer: Increase R30; 30 ÷ 150 × 100 = 20%. 3. A weekly expense is R50. Estimate its cost over a stated four-week month. Answer: R200 for four weeks. A calendar month is not always exactly four weeks."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: tariffs and financial documents. A tariff may have a fixed fee plus a charge per unit. Write down both parts before calculating. Use the same usage and billing period for each option. A cheaper per-unit rate may still cost more at low usage. A mathematical break-even value is a comparison tool; actual contracts may also have limits, taxes and once-off fees."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: tariffs and financial documents. Which plan costs less at 8 GB? At what usage do the bills match? A costs 50 + 10 × 8 = R130. B costs 20 × 8 = R160. At break-even, 50 + 10u = 20u, so u = 5 GB and both cost R100. Below 5 GB B is cheaper; above 5 GB A is cheaper, under these simplified tariff rules."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: tariffs and financial documents. 1. Calculate A at 12 GB. Answer: R50 + R120 = R170. 2. How much is saved using A instead of B at 12 GB? Answer: B costs R240, so the saving is R70. 3. Why should a buyer still inspect the full contract? Answer: Possible fair-use limits, coverage, fees, expiry and contract terms may affect the decision."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: interest and inflation. Simple interest is calculated on the original principal. Compound interest adds interest to the balance before the next period. For annual compounding use A = P(1 + i)ⁿ, with i as a decimal and n in years. A different compounding period needs a matching rate and number of periods. Inflation describes rising prices. A higher money balance does not guarantee greater purchasing power; compare growth over the same period."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: interest and inflation. Calculate the balance on R2 000 at 8% compounded annually for two years. P = 2 000, i = 0.08 and n = 2. A = 2 000(1.08)² = R2 332.80. Interest earned = 2 332.80 − 2 000 = R332.80. This example excludes fees and tax."}, {"pack_id": "matlit-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Finance: interest and inflation. 1. Calculate simple interest on R2 000 at 8% for two years. Answer: I = 2 000 × 0.08 × 2 = R320; total R2 320. 2. A basket rises from R500 to R540. Calculate inflation for that basket. Answer: 40 ÷ 500 × 100 = 8%. 3. If income stays R5 000 while prices rise, what happens to purchasing power? Answer: It falls: the same income buys fewer goods, assuming the price changes apply to those goods."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: instantaneous speed and velocity; acceleration. Distance is total path length, a scalar. Displacement is final position minus initial position, a vector with a chosen positive direction. Average velocity equals displacement divided by elapsed time. Average speed uses total distance instead. On a position–time graph, gradient represents velocity. A horizontal line means the position is constant, not necessarily zero."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: instantaneous speed and velocity; acceleration. The walker moves 4 m east in 2 s, then 4 m west in 2 s. Find average speed and velocity. Distance = 4 + 4 = 8 m; displacement = 0 − 0 = 0 m. Elapsed time = 4 s, so average speed = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 m·s⁻¹. Average velocity = 0 ÷ 4 = 0 m·s⁻¹. Motion occurred even though net displacement is zero."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: instantaneous speed and velocity; acceleration. 1. A cyclist travels 30 m east in 5 s. Find average velocity. Answer: 6 m·s⁻¹ east. 2. Velocity changes from 2 to 8 m·s⁻¹ east in 3 s. Find acceleration. Answer: a = (8 − 2)/3 = 2 m·s⁻² east. 3. Explain the gradient of a downward-sloping position–time line. Answer: Velocity is negative relative to the chosen positive direction; it does not automatically mean slowing down."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: Newton’s laws and application. A free-body diagram isolates one object and shows forces acting on it, not forces it exerts on other objects. Choose a positive direction. Resolve and add forces along that axis before applying Fnet = ma. On a horizontal surface with no vertical acceleration, normal force balances weight if there are no other vertical forces. Friction acts against relative motion or its tendency."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: Newton’s laws and application. Find the horizontal acceleration of the crate. Use g = 9.8 m·s⁻². Take right as positive. Vertical resultant = 49 − 49 = 0 N. Horizontal resultant = 20 − 5 = 15 N right. a = Fnet/m = 15/5 = 3 m·s⁻² right."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: Newton’s laws and application. 1. A 4 kg trolley has a net force of 12 N left. Find acceleration. Answer: 3 m·s⁻² left. 2. What resultant force keeps an object at constant velocity? Answer: Zero resultant force; individual forces may still act. 3. Are weight and normal force a Newton’s third-law pair? Explain. Answer: No. They act on the same object. A third-law pair acts on two different objects."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: momentum and impulse. Momentum p = mv is a vector. Select a positive direction and give opposite velocities opposite signs. The total momentum of an isolated system is conserved. In a collision, use the condition of negligible net external impulse. Impulse equals change in momentum: FnetΔt = Δp. Momentum conservation does not imply kinetic energy conservation in an inelastic collision."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: momentum and impulse. A 2 kg trolley moving right at 3 m·s⁻¹ sticks to a stationary 1 kg trolley. Find their final velocity. Before: total momentum = 2(3) + 1(0) = 6 kg·m·s⁻¹. After: the combined mass is 3 kg, so 6 = 3v and v = 2 m·s⁻¹ right. Initial kinetic energy is 9 J; final kinetic energy is 6 J. The 3 J difference becomes other energy forms."}, {"pack_id": "phsc-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Mechanics: momentum and impulse. 1. Find the momentum of a 0.5 kg ball moving left at 4 m·s⁻¹, taking right as positive. Answer: −2 kg·m·s⁻¹. 2. It rebounds right at 6 m·s⁻¹. Find Δp. Answer: Final momentum = +3; change = 3 − (−2) = +5 kg·m·s⁻¹. 3. If contact lasts 0.1 s, find the average net force. Answer: Fnet = 5/0.1 = 50 N right."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life at the molecular, cellular and tissue level: cells. Cells are the basic structural and functional units of living organisms. A cell membrane controls movement of substances into and out of the cell. The nucleus contains genetic material. Mitochondria are sites of aerobic cellular respiration; they transfer energy into usable chemical forms. Typical plant cells have a cellulose cell wall and a large central vacuole. Photosynthetic plant cells contain chloroplasts; not every plant cell has chloroplasts."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life at the molecular, cellular and tissue level: cells. A micrograph shows a rigid outer layer and many chloroplasts. Explain the most likely cell type. The rigid outer layer is consistent with a cell wall. Chloroplasts support photosynthesis, indicating a photosynthetic plant cell. Use the observed structures as evidence; a rectangular shape alone is not enough to identify a cell reliably."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life at the molecular, cellular and tissue level: cells. 1. Name the structure that controls movement across a cell boundary. Answer: The cell membrane. 2. Explain why muscle cells need many mitochondria. Answer: Muscle contraction needs energy; mitochondria support aerobic respiration and ATP production. 3. A drawing is 40 mm long and the actual cell is 0.1 mm. Calculate magnification. Answer: Magnification = drawing size / actual size = 40/0.1 = 400 times."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life processes in plants and animals: photosynthesis. Photosynthesis uses light energy to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen. Chlorophyll absorbs light in chloroplasts. A limiting factor restricts the rate even when other requirements are available. Increasing light may eventually stop increasing the rate if another factor becomes limiting. A fair investigation changes one independent variable, measures a dependent variable and controls other relevant conditions. Repeat measurements to improve reliability."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life processes in plants and animals: photosynthesis. Interpret the results and identify one weakness of counting bubbles. As lamp distance increases, bubble count falls, suggesting reduced photosynthesis as light decreases. Distance is changed; bubble count is measured. Keep plant size, water temperature and carbon dioxide availability controlled. Bubble sizes vary, so counts are an imperfect estimate of oxygen volume. Measuring gas volume over time gives a stronger comparison."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Life processes in plants and animals: photosynthesis. 1. Name two raw materials of photosynthesis. Answer: Carbon dioxide and water. 2. Why might a lamp heat confound the investigation? Answer: Temperature also affects enzyme-controlled reactions, so the result may reflect two changing variables. 3. Why does a rate plateau not mean that photosynthesis has stopped? Answer: The rate can remain positive but constant because another factor limits further increase."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Genetics and inheritance: monohybrid crosses. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene. In a simple complete-dominance model, one dominant allele is enough for the dominant phenotype. Genotype describes the allele combination; phenotype is the observable characteristic. A heterozygote has two different alleles. Each fertilisation is a new probability event. Expected ratios describe many offspring, not a guaranteed sequence within one family."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Genetics and inheritance: monohybrid crosses. In a hypothetical plant, T is dominant for tall growth and t is recessive for short growth. Cross two heterozygotes. Parents: Tt × Tt. Each produces T and t gametes. The combinations are TT, Tt, Tt and tt: genotype ratio 1 : 2 : 1. Tall offspring probability = 3/4; short = 1/4. Phenotype ratio is 3 tall : 1 short under the stated model."}, {"pack_id": "lisc-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Genetics and inheritance: monohybrid crosses. 1. Cross Tt with tt and list the offspring genotypes. Answer: Tt, tt, Tt, tt: 1 Tt : 1 tt. 2. State the probability of short offspring in that cross. Answer: 1/2, or 50%. 3. Explain why two tall parents can produce a short offspring. Answer: Both may be heterozygous and each may contribute a recessive t allele, producing tt."}, {"pack_id": "acc-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: accounting equation and double entry. The accounting equation is Assets = Owner’s equity + Liabilities. Every transaction must preserve this relationship. Assets are controlled resources; liabilities are obligations. Capital contributed increases owner’s equity; drawings decrease it. Buying an asset for cash changes the mix of assets without necessarily changing total assets. A loan increases both cash and liabilities."}, {"pack_id": "acc-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: accounting equation and double entry. Calculate the balances after all three transactions in the table. Cash = 10 000 − 3 000 + 2 000 = R9 000; equipment = R3 000. Total assets = R12 000. Equity = R10 000 and liabilities = R2 000. Check: 12 000 = 10 000 + 2 000. Equipment was not an expense in this purchase."}, {"pack_id": "acc-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: accounting equation and double entry. 1. The owner withdraws R500 cash for personal use. State the equation effects. Answer: Assets decrease R500; equity decreases R500 through drawings; liabilities unchanged. 2. The business pays R200 cash for electricity. State the effects. Answer: Assets and equity each decrease R200; electricity is an expense. 3. Why is a bank loan not income? Answer: It must be repaid and creates a liability, rather than earned revenue."}, {"pack_id": "acc-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: partnerships. In a school partnership appropriation calculation, partners’ salaries and interest on capital allocate profit according to the agreement. Deduct the agreed appropriations from net profit to find residual profit. Divide the residual using the agreed profit-sharing ratio. A partner’s drawings are not a business operating expense. Keep capital, current accounts and cash movements distinct."}, {"pack_id": "acc-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: partnerships. Net profit is R100 000. Use the table to calculate each partner’s total appropriation. Total salary and interest = 30 000 + 20 000 + 5 000 + 3 000 = R58 000. Residual = 100 000 − 58 000 = R42 000. A gets 3/5 × 42 000 = R25 200; B gets R16 800. A total = R60 200; B total = R39 800. Their totals reconcile to R100 000."}, {"pack_id": "acc-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: partnerships. 1. Calculate 10% interest on capital of R80 000 for a full year. Answer: R8 000. 2. Split residual profit R25 000 in the ratio 2 : 3. Answer: R10 000 and R15 000 respectively. 3. Why should partner drawings not reduce net profit? Answer: Drawings are withdrawals by owners, not costs incurred to earn business revenue."}, {"pack_id": "acc-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: companies; analysis and interpretation. Gross profit is sales less cost of sales. Gross profit margin = gross profit / sales × 100; mark-up on cost uses cost of sales as denominator. Current ratio = current assets / current liabilities. Acid-test ratio excludes inventory from current assets in the simplified school calculation. Ratios need comparison with prior periods, targets or context. A high current ratio can include slow-moving stock and does not automatically mean cash is available."}, {"pack_id": "acc-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: companies; analysis and interpretation. Calculate gross profit margin, current ratio and acid-test ratio. Gross profit = 500 000 − 300 000 = R200 000; margin = 200 000/500 000 × 100 = 40%. Current ratio = 120 000 : 60 000 = 2 : 1. Acid-test = (120 000 − 40 000) : 60 000 = 1.33 : 1, rounded to two decimals."}, {"pack_id": "acc-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Financial accounting: companies; analysis and interpretation. 1. Calculate the mark-up on cost for the same company. Answer: 200 000/300 000 × 100 = 66.67%. 2. If inventory rises while cash falls by the same amount, what happens to current ratio? Answer: It is unchanged if total current assets and liabilities stay the same, although asset quality changes. 3. Give one reason acid-test ratio may still overstate immediate cash availability. Answer: Debtors may pay late or not pay; receivables are not immediately available cash."}, {"pack_id": "bst-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: micro, market and macro. The micro environment is inside the business: its vision, resources and functions. Management usually has substantial control over it. The market environment includes customers, suppliers and competitors. A business can influence relationships but cannot fully control these parties. The macro environment includes wider economic, social, technological and other external forces. Businesses usually adapt to these rather than control them."}, {"pack_id": "bst-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: micro, market and macro. A stationery shop loses sales because staff arrive late and a competitor offers delivery. Classify and respond. Late staff: micro environment. The owner can set attendance expectations and improve scheduling. Competitor delivery: market environment. The owner can investigate customer needs and offer a feasible delivery option. Explain why the response fits the cause instead of giving the same generic recommendation for both."}, {"pack_id": "bst-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: micro, market and macro. 1. Classify a new internal staff training programme. Answer: Micro: internal skills and staffing. 2. Classify a new competing shop. Answer: Market: a competitor operates in the external market. 3. Why can a small shop not simply eliminate economy-wide inflation? Answer: Inflation is a wider economic influence beyond the individual firm’s control; the firm must adapt."}, {"pack_id": "bst-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business ventures: creative thinking and problem solving. Problem solving begins by defining a specific problem and gathering evidence. A fall in sales is a symptom until its causes are investigated. Generate alternatives before selecting one. Compare likely benefits, costs, time and risks instead of choosing the first idea. Implementation needs an owner, deadline and measurable result. Review evidence afterwards and adjust if the solution does not work."}, {"pack_id": "bst-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business ventures: creative thinking and problem solving. A small business has 20 late orders, 15 caused by stock-outs. Recommend a justified first intervention. The evidence points to stock availability as the main measured cause, not necessarily unmotivated staff. Compare a reorder-level system with holding much more stock. The first may reduce cash tied up but needs accurate records. Assign the stock controller a two-week trial and compare the late-order count, while checking demand changes."}, {"pack_id": "bst-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business ventures: creative thinking and problem solving. 1. Why should customer complaints be analysed before selecting a solution? Answer: To identify patterns and causes instead of guessing from isolated symptoms. 2. Give one disadvantage of carrying excess stock. Answer: It ties up cash and risks damage, obsolescence or storage costs. 3. Name a measurable indicator for a trial delivery improvement. Answer: Percentage of orders delivered on time over a stated period, compared with a baseline."}, {"pack_id": "bst-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: business strategies; SWOT analysis. Strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external. A SWOT list should use specific evidence. Strategy responds to the business situation. Options may include development, integration, diversification or defensive measures, depending on the case. An exam response needs a clear recommendation and application to the scenario. Consider affordability, risk and likely outcomes rather than assuming expansion always works."}, {"pack_id": "bst-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: business strategies; SWOT analysis. The publisher has strong authors but slow delivery and little cash. Recommend a cautious response to demand for digital notes. Use the author strength to pilot a small digital product rather than attempting every subject immediately. A pilot can reduce delivery delays and limit upfront costs, but requires editing and reliable access. Evaluate download completion, learner feedback and contribution to costs before expanding."}, {"pack_id": "bst-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Business environments: business strategies; SWOT analysis. 1. Classify a cash shortage in SWOT. Answer: Weakness: an internal resource limitation. 2. Classify a new competitor offering free content. Answer: Threat: an external competitive influence. 3. Give two criteria for evaluating the digital pilot. Answer: For example, completed downloads and learner usefulness ratings; also compare costs with revenue or funding."}, {"pack_id": "eco-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow and economic participants. In a simple closed two-sector model, households supply factors of production and firms produce goods and services. Factor payments such as wages flow from firms to households. Consumption spending flows from households to firms. Real flows and money flows move in opposite directions. This simplified model excludes government, financial and foreign sectors; real economies include them."}, {"pack_id": "eco-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow and economic participants. A learner’s household earns R4 000 from a bakery and spends R600 on bread. Identify the flows. Labour supplied to the bakery is a real factor flow from household to firm. The R4 000 wage is a money flow from firm to household. Bread moves from firm to household, while the R600 spending moves in the opposite direction."}, {"pack_id": "eco-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow and economic participants. 1. Name two factors of production besides labour. Answer: Land, capital or entrepreneurship; any two. 2. Is a wage a real flow or a money flow? Answer: A money flow: payment for labour. 3. Explain why lower household spending may affect firms. Answer: It can reduce firms’ sales revenue, which may affect production and demand for factors."}, {"pack_id": "eco-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Microeconomics: price elasticity; relationships between markets. Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a price change, other things equal. Use the magnitude of percentage change in quantity divided by percentage change in price. Using original-value percentages, an elasticity greater than 1 is elastic; less than 1 is inelastic; equal to 1 is unit elastic. State the method used because different percentage conventions can produce different results. Markets are linked. A rise in a key input price can increase production costs and reduce product supply, other things equal. A product’s own price change is a movement along its demand curve."}, {"pack_id": "eco-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Microeconomics: price elasticity; relationships between markets. Use the table to calculate price elasticity of demand and explain the revenue change. Price change = (11 − 10)/10 × 100 = 10%. Quantity change = (80 − 100)/100 × 100 = −20%. Elasticity magnitude = 20/10 = 2. Demand is elastic over this change using the stated method. Revenue falls from 10 × 100 = R1 000 to 11 × 80 = R880 because the proportional quantity fall exceeds the price increase."}, {"pack_id": "eco-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Microeconomics: price elasticity; relationships between markets. 1. Price rises 5% and quantity demanded falls 2%. Calculate elasticity magnitude. Answer: 2/5 = 0.4. 2. Classify the demand response in question 1. Answer: Inelastic, because the elasticity magnitude is less than 1. 3. Explain how a rise in paper prices can affect notebook supply. Answer: Paper is an input. Higher input costs can reduce notebook supply, shifting its supply curve left, other things equal."}, {"pack_id": "eco-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow; the multiplier. The marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is the share of additional income spent on consumption. In the simple model MPS = 1 − MPC. The simple closed-economy multiplier is k = 1/(1 − MPC). An initial autonomous expenditure change has a modelled total income effect ΔY = kΔA. This is a simplified model without taxes or imports and with capacity to expand output. It does not promise that any real policy achieves the calculated result."}, {"pack_id": "eco-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow; the multiplier. MPC is 0.8 and autonomous investment increases by R20 million. Calculate the simple income effect. MPS = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2. k = 1/0.2 = 5. Therefore ΔY = 5 × R20 million = R100 million. The result includes the original injection and later spending rounds; it is conditional on the model assumptions."}, {"pack_id": "eco-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Macroeconomics: circular flow; the multiplier. 1. Calculate k when MPC = 0.75. Answer: k = 1/(1 − 0.75) = 4. 2. Calculate the effect of a R10 million injection using that k. Answer: ΔY = 4 × 10 = R40 million. 3. How would additional import leakages tend to affect the multiplier? Answer: They tend to reduce the domestic income multiplier because some spending goes abroad."}, {"pack_id": "tour-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tourism sectors; domestic tourism. A domestic tourist travels within their country of residence outside their usual environment. International tourism crosses a national border. Tourism relies on connected sectors such as transport, accommodation, attractions, food services and travel distribution. Good planning considers accessibility, safety, realistic travel time and visitors’ needs. Do not assume all tourists have the same budget or physical access requirements."}, {"pack_id": "tour-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tourism sectors; domestic tourism. A family living in Gauteng visits a museum in the Western Cape and stays two nights. Classify the trip and identify three sectors. The trip is domestic because residence and destination are within South Africa. Transport gets the family there; accommodation provides the overnight stay; the museum is an attraction. Check opening times and travel distance before promising an itinerary; actual prices and schedules must be verified."}, {"pack_id": "tour-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tourism sectors; domestic tourism. 1. A resident of Botswana visits South Africa. Is this domestic tourism for South Africa? Answer: No; it is inbound international tourism for South Africa. 2. Name a tourism service supplied by a travel agent. Answer: For example, arranging transport and accommodation bookings. 3. Give one way a local tourism business can support its community. Answer: Employ locally, source suitable goods locally or partner fairly with community guides."}, {"pack_id": "tour-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tour planning; responsible and sustainable tourism. An itinerary sequences travel, activities and rest with realistic buffers. Check actual access, opening times and booking requirements. A budget separates per-person costs from shared costs. State the number of travellers, nights and any exclusions. Responsible tourism aims to reduce harm and support local benefits while providing a good visitor experience. Claims should be tied to specific actions, not vague green labels."}, {"pack_id": "tour-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tour planning; responsible and sustainable tourism. Calculate the total and per-person cost, then suggest one responsible choice. Total = 600 + 800 + 240 + 300 = R1 940. Shared equally, cost per person = 1 940/2 = R970; this excludes any unstated fees. Use an appropriately qualified local guide and follow site rules; explain how the choice supports livelihoods or protects the destination."}, {"pack_id": "tour-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Tour planning; responsible and sustainable tourism. 1. Add a 10% contingency to the total. Answer: R194 contingency; revised total R2 134. 2. Why must a four-hour journey not be scheduled as a one-hour transfer? Answer: It makes later activities infeasible and may encourage unsafe rushing. 3. Name one measurable action that reduces waste. Answer: For example, provide refillable water bottles and measure fewer single-use bottles used."}, {"pack_id": "tour-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Map work and tour planning: time zones and travel calculations. UTC offsets state how far local time is ahead of or behind UTC. Convert departure time to UTC first, then add elapsed flight time. Convert the arrival UTC time to the destination’s stated offset. Adjust the date whenever the calculation crosses midnight. Use the offsets supplied in an exam. For real bookings verify the actual date-specific local time, because some destinations use seasonal clock changes."}, {"pack_id": "tour-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Map work and tour planning: time zones and travel calculations. A flight leaves Johannesburg at 22:00 on Monday, flies 10 hours and arrives at a destination on UTC+1. Find arrival time and day. Departure is Monday 20:00 UTC. Add 10 hours: Tuesday 06:00 UTC. Add the destination’s 1-hour offset: Tuesday 07:00 local time."}, {"pack_id": "tour-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Map work and tour planning: time zones and travel calculations. 1. A city is UTC−3. What is its time when UTC is 15:00? Answer: 12:00 on the same day. 2. A flight departs there at 12:00 and lasts 8 hours. Find arrival UTC. Answer: Departure 15:00 UTC; arrival 23:00 UTC the same day. 3. Convert that arrival to Johannesburg at UTC+2. Answer: 01:00 the next day."}, {"pack_id": "hist-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The French Revolution: causes and historical skills. Before 1789, French society was divided into estates with unequal privileges. Social inequality and debates about political authority contributed to criticism of the old order. State debt and a fiscal crisis increased pressure for reform. Harvest failures and high bread prices intensified popular hardship. A revolution has interacting causes. A single event can trigger action without explaining the longer processes that made it possible."}, {"pack_id": "hist-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The French Revolution: causes and historical skills. Explain how financial crisis and inequality could combine to weaken the monarchy. Debt and difficulties raising revenue made the government seek fiscal and political solutions. Unequal privileges made reform contentious and encouraged demands for fairer representation and taxation. Connect these pressures: a financial problem opened a political confrontation in an already unequal society."}, {"pack_id": "hist-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The French Revolution: causes and historical skills. 1. Name one long-term social cause. Answer: The unequal privileges and burdens of the estate system. 2. Why is the storming of the Bastille insufficient as the only explanation for revolution? Answer: It describes an event but does not explain the prior social, fiscal and political conditions. 3. What should you check before using a political cartoon as evidence? Answer: Creator, date, intended audience, purpose, symbols and what the cartoon can and cannot show."}, {"pack_id": "hist-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Communism in Russia, 1900–1940: revolutions of 1917. Russia’s involvement in the First World War intensified military, economic and political pressures. Food shortages and discontent weakened confidence in tsarist rule. The February Revolution led to the abdication of Nicholas II. The Provisional Government faced continuing war and competing political authority. In the October Revolution the Bolsheviks took power. Explaining this outcome requires attention to government weaknesses, political organisation and popular demands."}, {"pack_id": "hist-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Communism in Russia, 1900–1940: revolutions of 1917. Explain why ending tsarist rule did not immediately resolve Russia’s crisis. The February Revolution changed political leadership but inherited war and shortages. Continuing involvement in the war sustained hardship and discontent. Rival centres of authority and competing programmes made effective government difficult; political change alone did not remove the underlying pressures."}, {"pack_id": "hist-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Communism in Russia, 1900–1940: revolutions of 1917. 1. Identify the ruler who abdicated in 1917. Answer: Nicholas II. 2. Why should a historian compare a Bolshevik slogan with other evidence? Answer: A slogan reveals political aims and persuasion, but not by itself actual support or implementation. 3. Give one reason not to merge February and October into a single event. Answer: They removed different authorities and occurred in different political circumstances."}, {"pack_id": "hist-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Civil resistance in South Africa, 1970s–1980s. The Soweto uprising began on 16 June 1976 amid opposition to apartheid education, including the imposition of Afrikaans as a medium of instruction. Black Consciousness encouraged psychological liberation and black self-reliance. Explain its influence without reducing a broad movement to one slogan. Civil resistance involved multiple organisations, communities and methods. Historical arguments should show change over time and recognise different forms of mobilisation."}, {"pack_id": "hist-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Civil resistance in South Africa, 1970s–1980s. Plan a paragraph on why education became a focus of resistance in 1976. Make a claim: unequal apartheid education and the language policy provoked student opposition. Support it with relevant contextual evidence, including student mobilisation and the events beginning on 16 June. Explain how the evidence supports the claim; do not imply that language was the only grievance or that all participants had identical motives."}, {"pack_id": "hist-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Civil resistance in South Africa, 1970s–1980s. 1. Give the date associated with the start of the Soweto uprising. Answer: 16 June 1976. 2. Name one limitation of using a single later oral-history interview. Answer: Memory, later experience and the interview setting may shape the account; corroboration is needed. 3. What distinguishes an argument from a list of events? Answer: An argument makes a defensible claim, selects relevant evidence and explains its significance."}, {"pack_id": "geo-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The atmosphere: heating of the atmosphere. The Sun supplies mainly shortwave radiation. Some is reflected; some is absorbed by the atmosphere and surface. The warmed Earth emits longwave infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit some outgoing infrared, helping warm the lower atmosphere and surface. Albedo is reflectivity. A brighter surface generally reflects more incoming radiation, while a darker surface generally absorbs more under comparable conditions."}, {"pack_id": "geo-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The atmosphere: heating of the atmosphere. Why might dark tar warm more than a pale painted surface in sunshine? Compare surfaces under the same sunlight and other similar conditions. Dark tar generally has a lower albedo and absorbs a larger share of incoming radiation. More absorbed energy can raise its temperature; wind, moisture and material properties also affect actual temperatures."}, {"pack_id": "geo-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: The atmosphere: heating of the atmosphere. 1. Which has higher albedo: fresh snow or dark tar? Answer: Fresh snow. 2. Name the radiation mainly emitted by the Earth. Answer: Longwave infrared radiation. 3. Why is the greenhouse effect different from ozone depletion? Answer: The greenhouse effect concerns infrared absorption and re-emission; ozone depletion concerns reduced protection from harmful ultraviolet radiation."}, {"pack_id": "geo-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development geography: indicators and development. Development involves changes in living conditions and opportunities, not only economic output. GDP per capita divides output by population. It is an average and does not describe how income is distributed. Use several indicators, such as life expectancy, education and access to services. Compare the same year, units and methods before drawing conclusions."}, {"pack_id": "geo-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development geography: indicators and development. Can you conclude that A is more developed simply because its GDP per capita is higher? A has the higher measured average output per person. B has higher life expectancy and water access in this invented dataset. A single indicator cannot justify the full conclusion. Assess distribution, social outcomes and data quality before comparing development."}, {"pack_id": "geo-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development geography: indicators and development. 1. Output is R600 million and population 10 000. Find output per person. Answer: R60 000 per person. 2. Why can two places with equal GDP per capita have different living standards? Answer: Distribution, public services, costs and other conditions may differ. 3. Name one limitation of comparing indicators from different years. Answer: Economic or demographic changes can make the figures non-comparable over time."}, {"pack_id": "geo-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Geomorphology: fluvial processes; river profiles. A river’s long profile shows elevation along its course. It commonly becomes gentler downstream, although local geology and disturbances create exceptions. Erosion removes material, transport moves it and deposition occurs when the river can no longer carry part of its load. In a meander, faster flow commonly erodes the outer bank while slower flow deposits material on the inner bank. Distinguish this cross-sectional pattern from the river’s long profile."}, {"pack_id": "geo-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Geomorphology: fluvial processes; river profiles. Explain why the outside and inside banks of a meander may change differently. Flow is commonly faster and deeper near the outer bank, promoting erosion and a river cliff. Slower flow near the inner bank encourages deposition and a slip-off slope. Over time this can move the meander laterally. Real outcomes also depend on discharge, sediment and bank material."}, {"pack_id": "geo-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Geomorphology: fluvial processes; river profiles. 1. Define deposition. Answer: The laying down of sediment when transport capacity is insufficient to carry that load. 2. A river falls 120 m over 6 km. Calculate average gradient as a ratio in the same units. Answer: 120 : 6 000 = 1 : 50. 3. Why does gentler gradient not always mean lower discharge? Answer: Discharge depends on cross-sectional area and velocity; tributaries may increase volume downstream."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: comprehension; language in context. Literal questions ask for information stated directly. Inference combines a textual clue with a reasonable explanation; it is not unsupported guessing. A quotation supports a point only when you explain how its wording matters. Keep quotations short and relevant. Tone is the speaker’s attitude. Use the language of the passage to justify your description rather than choosing a tone word from memory."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: comprehension; language in context. Original text: “Lindi checked the empty road again. When the bus appeared, she finally exhaled and stepped into its warm light.” Explain Lindi’s likely feelings. The word “again” suggests repeated checking, supporting an inference of anxious waiting. “Finally exhaled” suggests relief when the bus arrives. “Warm light” gives the ending a comforting mood. The text does not establish why she was waiting or where she is going."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: comprehension; language in context. 1. What appears on the road? Answer: A bus. 2. Explain the effect of “again”. Answer: It suggests repeated action and can emphasise anticipation or worry. 3. Does the text prove that the bus was late? Justify. Answer: No. It shows waiting but gives no timetable or expected arrival time."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: critical language awareness. A persuasive text tries to influence an audience. Identify the intended audience, purpose and specific choices used to persuade. An assertion states a claim. Evidence provides a basis for judging it; check whether it is relevant and credible. Loaded language and rhetorical questions can shape emotion. Explain the likely effect in this particular text rather than merely naming a technique."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: critical language awareness. Explain how the poster encourages community participation. “Our” includes readers in a shared community and frames the library as a collective responsibility. The contrast between “fade” and “open” moves from possible loss to opportunity. The clear instruction identifies an action, but readers still need practical details such as place, time and acceptable donations."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: critical language awareness. 1. Identify the poster’s main request. Answer: Join Saturday’s book drive. 2. Is “a hundred doors” reliable numerical evidence? Explain. Answer: No. It is figurative language expressing opportunity, not a measured outcome. 3. Suggest one practical improvement to the poster. Answer: Add a verified venue, time and contact or donation requirements."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: literary response; writing and presenting. A literary response develops a reading of a text. Start with a precise claim that can be defended using its language or structure. Evidence must be followed by analysis: explain what a word, contrast, image or structural choice contributes to meaning. For prescribed literature, use the school’s current text and edition. This original short poem is skills practice and is not presented as a prescribed poem."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: literary response; writing and presenting. Write a paragraph explaining how the poem presents beginning a journey. Claim: the poem presents beginning as a choice to act despite uncertainty. Evidence: folding the map ends preparation, but “the road remains”, showing that the journey is still ahead. Analysis: the personified morning “waits” makes possibility feel available; the open ending does not guarantee success."}, {"pack_id": "enhl-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Reading and viewing: literary response; writing and presenting. 1. Identify one example of personification. Answer: “The morning waits.” 2. Explain the contrast between the first two lines. Answer: The map can be folded and preparation ended, but the actual road and journey still exist. 3. Why is “the speaker will definitely succeed” too strong? Answer: The poem offers possibility but gives no outcome; that claim exceeds its evidence."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: begrip; taal in konteks. Letterlike vrae vra na inligting wat direk in die teks staan. ’n Afleiding verbind ’n leidraad met ’n logiese gevolgtrekking. Haal kort en doelgerig aan. Verduidelik waarom die aangehaalde woord jou antwoord ondersteun. Toon beskryf die spreker se houding. Kies ’n gepaste beskrywing en bewys dit uit die teks."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: begrip; taal in konteks. Oorspronklike teks: “Mia kyk weer na die leë straat. Toe die bus verskyn, haal sy uiteindelik diep asem en stap die warm lig binne.” Hoe verander haar gevoel? “Weer” dui daarop dat Mia herhaaldelik kyk; dit kan afwagting of spanning suggereer. “Uiteindelik” en die diep asemhaling ondersteun ’n gevoel van verligting. “Warm lig” skep ’n gevoel van geborgenheid. Die teks sê nie waarom sy op die bus wag nie."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: begrip; taal in konteks. 1. Wat verskyn in die straat? Answer: ’n Bus. 2. Wat suggereer “weer”? Answer: Dat Mia al voorheen gekyk het; dit beklemtoon haar afwagting. 3. Bewys die teks dat die bus laat was? Motiveer. Answer: Nee. Geen verwagte aankomstyd word gegee nie."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: kritiese taalbewustheid. ’n Oorredende teks probeer die leser se houding of optrede beïnvloed. Bepaal die doel en teikengehoor. ’n Bewering is nie vanself bewys nie. Vra watter betroubare inligting die bewering ondersteun. Emotiewe taal en beeldspraak kan gevoelens beïnvloed. Verduidelik die uitwerking in die spesifieke konteks."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: kritiese taalbewustheid. Hoe probeer die plakkaat mense tot deelname oorhaal? “Ons” betrek die leser by die gemeenskap en biblioteek. Die moontlike verlies word gekontrasteer met die geleenthede wat boeke bied. Die opdrag is duidelik, maar die plek en tyd moet bygevoeg word sodat mense kan deelneem."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: kritiese taalbewustheid. 1. Wat moet die leser doen? Answer: Saterdag ’n boek skenk. 2. Is “honderd deure” ’n bewese statistiek? Verduidelik. Answer: Nee. Dit is figuurlik en stel baie geleenthede voor; geen telling word bewys nie. 3. Noem een praktiese verbetering. Answer: Voeg ’n bevestigde plek, tyd of kontakbesonderhede by."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: literêre ontleding; skryf en aanbied. ’n Literêre paragraaf begin met ’n duidelike stelling oor die teks. Kies relevante bewyse en ontleed dit. ’n Kort aanhaling vervang nie jou verduideliking nie. Bespreek die betekenis van die spesifieke woord of beeld. Gebruik jou skool se voorgeskrewe werke vir eksamenvoorbereiding. Hierdie oorspronklike oefengedig is nie ’n voorgeskrewe gedig nie."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: literêre ontleding; skryf en aanbied. Verduidelik hoe die gedig die toekoms as ’n moontlikheid voorstel. Stelling: die spreker kan begin beweeg, al is die uitkoms onbekend. Die kaart word gevou, maar die pad “bly oop”; voorbereiding en werklike optrede verskil. “Wag môre” personifieer die toekoms en skep afwagting, sonder om sukses te waarborg."}, {"pack_id": "afhl-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Lees en kyk: literêre ontleding; skryf en aanbied. 1. Identifiseer personifikasie. Answer: “Wag môre nog.” 2. Verduidelik die kontras in die eerste twee reëls. Answer: Die kaart word toegemaak, maar die pad en moontlikhede bly oop. 3. Waarom is ’n gewaarborgde gelukkige einde nie ’n veilige gevolgtrekking nie? Answer: Die gedig gee geen uitkoms nie; dit laat die toekoms oop."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqonda; ulwimi emxholweni. Imibuzo yolwazi oluthe ngqo ifuna into ebhalwe ngokucacileyo kwisicatshulwa. Uthelekelelo kufuneka luxhaswe zizikhokelo ezikwisicatshulwa. Xa ucaphula, khetha amagama ambalwa afanelekileyo. Cacisa indlela axhasa ngayo impendulo yakho. Musa ukongeza iziganeko ezingekhoyo. Yahlula into esiyaziyo kwinto esicinga ukuba inokwenzeka."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqonda; ulwimi emxholweni. Isicatshulwa sokuqala: “UNomsa wajonga indlela kwakhona. Ekugqibeleni yafika ibhasi. Wancuma waza wathatha ibhegi yakhe.” Yintoni ebonisa ukuba ebeyilindile ibhasi? Igama elithi “kwakhona” libonisa ukuba uNomsa ebesele ejonga indlela. “Ekugqibeleni” libonisa ukuphela kokulinda. Ukuncuma kwakhe xa ibhasi ifika kuxhasa uluvo lokuba uyavuya okanye ukhululekile."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqonda; ulwimi emxholweni. 1. Yintoni eyafikayo? Answer: Yibhasi. 2. Lithini igama elibonisa ukuphindwa kwesenzo? Answer: “Kwakhona”. 3. Ngaba siyazi ukuba uNomsa uya phi? Xhasa impendulo. Answer: Hayi. Isicatshulwa asiyichazi indawo aya kuyo."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetyenziswa kolwimi. Isibhengezo esicengayo sifuna ukutshintsha indlela abantu abacinga okanye abenza ngayo. Buza ukuba sithetha nabani kwaye sifuna benze ntoni. Amagama aquka wonke umntu anokwakha imvakalelo yobunye. Amagama achukumisa iimvakalelo anokwenza abantu baxhalabe okanye babe nethemba. Ibango alilingani nobungqina. Vavanya ukuba kukho ulwazi oluthembekileyo oluxhasa oko kuthethwayo."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetyenziswa kolwimi. Sicenga njani esi sibhengezo abantu ukuba bathathe inxaxheba? Igama elithi “lethu” libandakanya abantu kwithala leencwadi labo. Ibinzana elithi “masikhusele” libiza abantu ukuba basebenze kunye. “Iingcango ezininzi” limela amathuba okufunda; alithethi iingcango zesakhiwo kuphela."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetyenziswa kolwimi. 1. Yintoni ekufuneka abantu beze nayo? Answer: Incwadi. 2. Ngaba “iingcango ezininzi” libalo eliqinisekisiweyo? Cacisa. Answer: Hayi. Eli libinzana elifuziselayo elithetha amathuba. 3. Loluphi ulwazi olusebenzisekayo olungekhoyo? Answer: Umzekelo: indawo, ixesha elichanekileyo okanye iinkcukacha zoqhagamshelwano."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: uncwadi; ukubhala nokunikezela. Umhlathi wohlalutyo kufuneka ube noluvo, ubungqina kunye nengcaciso. Isicatshulwa masixhase into oyithethayo. Ukucaphula kuphela akwanelanga. Cacisa intsingiselo yamagama nendlela anxulumana ngayo noluvo lwakho. Lo mbongo wokuqala ngowokuziqhelanisa. Awuthathi indawo yeencwadi okanye imibongo emiselwe sisikolo sakho."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: uncwadi; ukubhala nokunikezela. Cacisa indlela lo mbongo obonisa ngayo ukuqala kohambo. Uluvo: isithethi sijonge amathuba angaphambili nangona isiphelo singaziwa. Ukusongwa kwemephu kubonisa ukuphela kokulungiselela, kodwa indlela isavulekile. Ukusa okulindileyo kunika ikamva isenzo somntu. Oku kudala ithemba, kungathembisi impumelelo eqinisekileyo."}, {"pack_id": "xhhl-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: uncwadi; ukubhala nokunikezela. 1. Chonga umzekelo wesimntwiso. Answer: “Kusasa kundilindile.” 2. Yahlula isithethi kumbhali. Answer: Isithethi lilizwi elithetha kumbongo; asisoloko singumbhali uqobo. 3. Ngaba lo mbongo uqinisekisa impumelelo? Xhasa impendulo. Answer: Hayi. Ubonisa ithuba lokuqala kodwa akasiniki isiphelo sohambo."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqondisisa; ulimi engqikithini. Imibuzo eqondile ifuna ulwazi olubhalwe ngokusobala. Isiphetho sidinga umkhondo ovela esiqeshini kanye nencazelo enengqondo. Uma ucaphuna, khetha amagama ambalwa afanele. Chaza ukuthi ahlobana kanjani nempendulo yakho. Ungaqambi izehlakalo ezingekho esiqeshini. Hlukanisa esikwaziyo kulokho esicabanga ukuthi kungenzeka."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqondisisa; ulimi engqikithini. Isiqeshana sokuqala: “UNomsa wabheka umgwaqo futhi. Ekugcineni lafika ibhasi. Wamamatheka wathatha isikhwama sakhe.” Yini ekhombisa ukuthi ubelindile? Igama elithi “futhi” likhombisa ukuthi uNomsa wayeseke wabheka umgwaqo. “Ekugcineni” likhombisa ukuphela kokulinda. Ukumamatheka kwakhe lapho ibhasi lifika kusekela umqondo wenjabulo noma wokukhululeka."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqondisisa; ulimi engqikithini. 1. Yini eyafikayo? Answer: Yibhasi. 2. Yiliphi igama elikhombisa ukuphindwa kwesenzo? Answer: “Futhi”. 3. Siyazi yini ukuthi uNomsa uyaphi? Sekela impendulo. Answer: Cha. Isiqeshana asiyisho indawo aya kuyo."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetshenziswa kolimi. Umbhalo onxenxayo uzama ukuthonya indlela abantu abacabanga noma abenza ngayo. Buza ukuthi ukhuluma nobani nokuthi ufuna benzeni. Amagama afaka wonke umuntu angakha ubunye. Ulimi oluthinta imizwa lungadala ukukhathazeka noma ithemba. Isimangalo asibona ubufakazi ngokwaso. Bheka ukuthi kukhona yini ulwazi oluthembekile olusisekelayo."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetshenziswa kolimi. Lesi sikhangiso sibanxenxa kanjani abantu ukuba babambe iqhaza? Igama elithi “wethu” lifaka abafundi emphakathini womtapo wezincwadi. “Masivikele” licela isenzo esihlanganyelwe. “Iminyango eminingi” imele amathuba okufunda; ayisho iminyango yesakhiwo kuphela."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: ukuqaphela ukusetshenziswa kolimi. 1. Yini okufanele abantu bayilethe? Answer: Incwadi. 2. Ingabe “iminyango eminingi” iyisibalo esiqinisekisiwe? Chaza. Answer: Cha. Kuyinkulumo engokomfanekiso emele amathuba. 3. Yiluphi ulwazi olusebenzisekayo olungekho? Answer: Isibonelo: indawo, isikhathi esiqondile noma imininingwane yokuxhumana."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: imibhalo yobuciko; ukubhala nokwethula. Isigaba sokuhlaziya sidinga umbono, ubufakazi kanye nencazelo. Khetha ubufakazi obuhambisana nombono wakho. Ukucaphuna akuthathi indawo yencazelo. Chaza ukuthi amagama noma izithombe zakha kanjani umqondo. Le nkondlo yokuqala ingeyokuzijwayeza. Ayithathi indawo yezincwadi nezinkondlo ezimiswe yisikole sakho."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: imibhalo yobuciko; ukubhala nokwethula. Chaza ukuthi inkondlo iveza kanjani ukuqala kohambo. Umbono: isikhulumi sibona ithuba lokuqala, nakuba umphumela ungaziwa. Ukugoqa ibalazwe kuqeda ukulungiselela, kodwa indlela ihlala ivulekile. Ukusa okulindile kunikezwa isenzo somuntu; lokhu kwakha ithemba ngaphandle kokuqinisekisa impumelelo."}, {"pack_id": "zuhl-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Ukufunda nokubukela: imibhalo yobuciko; ukubhala nokwethula. 1. Thola isibonelo sokwenzasamuntu. Answer: “Ukusa kungilindile.” 2. Hlukanisa isikhulumi nembongi. Answer: Isikhulumi yizwi elikhuluma enkondlweni; asihlali siyimbongi uqobo. 3. Ingabe inkondlo iqinisekisa impumelelo? Sekela impendulo. Answer: Cha. Inkondlo iveza amathuba kodwa ayisiniki isiphetho sohambo."}, {"pack_id": "lo-10", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development of the self in society: self-awareness and goal setting. A useful goal names a specific action and a realistic time frame. Link it to something you value rather than comparing yourself constantly with others. Planning includes barriers such as transport, data, responsibilities and access to a quiet space. Choose supports that fit your situation. Review what happened, identify an obstacle and adjust the plan. Reflection is evidence for a better next step, not proof of your worth as a person."}, {"pack_id": "lo-10", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development of the self in society: self-awareness and goal setting. A learner wants to “do better” but has limited data. Turn this into a workable one-week plan. Choose a small topic and download or print questions when Wi-Fi is available. Set two short practice sessions and a Friday answer check, allowing for other responsibilities. Ask a tutor or teacher about one specific error; adjust next week’s plan using the result."}, {"pack_id": "lo-10", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Development of the self in society: self-awareness and goal setting. 1. Rewrite “I will study everything tomorrow” as a realistic action. Answer: For example: complete three algebra questions after school on Tuesday and check them on Wednesday. 2. Name one support besides paid tutoring. Answer: A teacher, peer study group, school library or trusted adult. 3. What should you do if the first plan was unrealistic? Answer: Identify the barrier, reduce or reschedule the action and review again."}, {"pack_id": "lo-11", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Careers and career choices: requirements and opportunities. A career decision combines interests, strengths, values, subject choices and practical constraints. One test or mark should not define your whole future. Entry requirements differ by institution, programme and year. Check official sources for subject requirements, selection rules and deadlines. Meeting minimum requirements does not guarantee admission. Keep alternative pathways and investigate funding, accommodation and support."}, {"pack_id": "lo-11", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Careers and career choices: requirements and opportunities. A learner enjoys science but has not checked programme requirements. What should they do before applying? List two suitable programmes and identify each institution’s official admissions page. Record required subjects, marks, selection steps and the current application deadline with the date checked. Compare affordability and support, prepare documents and keep a realistic alternative pathway."}, {"pack_id": "lo-11", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Careers and career choices: requirements and opportunities. 1. Why are social-media posts insufficient as the only admissions source? Answer: Posts may be outdated, incomplete or unofficial; verify using the institution. 2. Distinguish eligibility from acceptance. Answer: Eligibility meets stated entry conditions; acceptance is the institution’s actual offer after selection. 3. Name two costs besides tuition to investigate. Answer: For example accommodation, transport, data, books or meals."}, {"pack_id": "lo-12", "page": 1, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Study skills: examination preparation and stress management. Retrieval practice means trying to recall or apply knowledge before checking notes. Checking errors afterwards helps choose the next revision task. A balanced revision plan rotates subjects, includes breaks and protects sleep. Long hours alone do not prove that learning has occurred. Stress can affect concentration. Use practical supports and talk to a trusted adult or qualified professional when distress is persistent or interferes with daily life."}, {"pack_id": "lo-12", "page": 1, "number": 2, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Study skills: examination preparation and stress management. A learner rereads notes for hours but struggles with questions. Suggest a stronger revision session. Choose a short topic and attempt a few questions before looking at the notes. Mark the attempt, classify errors as concept, calculation or interpretation, and explain one correction. Return to a similar question after a gap and ask for help if the concept remains unclear."}, {"pack_id": "lo-12", "page": 2, "number": 1, "content": "SmartBridge FET first edition; subject-teacher review pending. CAPS focus: Study skills: examination preparation and stress management. 1. Why is copying a memo not enough evidence of learning? Answer: It does not show that the learner can recall or apply the reasoning independently. 2. Name one adjustment that can reduce revision overload. Answer: Reduce session length, prioritise a specific topic, schedule breaks or seek help planning. 3. How can a learner use a teacher’s feedback? Answer: Identify a specific recurring error, practise the relevant skill and check the improvement."}]$chunks$::jsonb) as c(pack_id text,page integer,number integer,content text)
join public.resources r on r.metadata->>'smartbridge_pack_id'=c.pack_id
where r.is_approved=true and r.is_premium=false and coalesce(r.is_archived,false)=false
on conflict(source_type,source_id,page_number,chunk_number) do update set content=excluded.content,indexed_at=now();
